@str.50 = private unnamed_addr constant [5865 x i8] c"Runtime options for Pony programs (not for use with ponyc):\0A  --ponymaxthreads Use N scheduler threads. Defaults to the number of\0A                   cores (not hyperthreads) available.\0A                   This can't be larger than the number of cores available.\0A  --ponyminthreads Minimum number of active scheduler threads allowed.\0A                   Defaults to 0, meaning that all scheduler threads are\0A                   allowed to be suspended when no work is available.\0A                   This can't be larger than --ponymaxthreads if provided,\0A                   or the physical cores available\0A  --ponynoscale    Don't scale down the scheduler threads.\0A                   See --ponymaxthreads on how to specify the number of threads\0A                   explicitly. Can't be used with --ponyminthreads.\0A  --ponysuspendthreshold\0A                   Amount of idle time before a scheduler thread suspends\0A                   itself to minimize resource consumption (max 1000 ms,\0A                   min 1 ms).\0A                   Defaults to 1 ms.\0A  --ponycdinterval Run cycle detection every N ms (max 1000 ms, min 10 ms).\0A                   Defaults to 100 ms.\0A  --ponygcinitial  Defer garbage collection until an actor is using at\0A                   least 2^N bytes, where N is less than the machine's\0A                   pointer width in bits (at most 63 on 64-bit).\0A                   Defaults to 2^14.\0A  --ponygcfactor   After GC, an actor will next be GC'd at a heap memory\0A                   usage N times its current value. This is a floating\0A                   point value. Defaults to 2.0.\0A  --ponymemoryprofile\0A                   Trade the allocator's resident memory against throughput\0A                   on a scale from 1 to 10: 1 returns freed memory quickly\0A                   for the smallest footprint, 10 holds it for the most\0A                   throughput. Defaults to 3: the scale has little room below\0A                   it for less memory and much more above it for throughput,\0A                   so the balanced default sits low on it.\0A  --ponynoyield    Do not yield the CPU when no work is available.\0A  --ponynoblock    Do not send block messages to the cycle detector.\0A                   Turning this on with disable the cycle detector.\0A  --ponypin        Pin scheduler threads to CPU cores. The ASIO thread\0A                   can also be pinned if `--ponypinasio` is set.\0A  --ponypinasio    Pin the ASIO thread to a CPU the way scheduler\0A                   threads are pinned to CPUs. Requires `--ponypin` to\0A                   be set to have any effect.\0A  --ponypinpinnedactorthread\0A                   Pin the pinned actor thread to a CPU the way scheduler\0A                   threads are pinned to CPUs. Requires `--ponypin` to\0A                   be set to have any effect.\0A  --ponyprintstatsinterval\0A                   Print actor stats before an actor is destroyed and\0A                   print scheduler stats every X seconds. Defaults to -1 (never).\0A  --ponytracingmode\0A                   Mode for tracing. Valid options are:\0A                       file - write trace events to file\0A                       flight_recorder - save events to a per-thread in memory\0A                                         circular buffer in case of program error\0A                                         (SIGSEGV, SIGILL, SIGBUS, SIGFPE, etc)\0A                                         when they are written out\0A                   defaults to: file\0A  --ponytracingformat\0A                   Output format for tracing in file mode. Valid options are:\0A                       json - chromium trace json format\0A                   defaults to: json\0A  --ponytracingoutput\0A                   Output file for tracing in file mode. Valid options are:\0A                       - - stdout\0A                       ~ - stderr\0A                       <STRING> - filename/path to write to\0A                   defaults to: ponytrace.json\0A  --ponytracingcategories\0A                   Tracing categories to enable. Valid categories are:\0A                       actor - basic actor events\0A                       actor_behavior - actor behavior run events\0A                       actor_gc - actor garbage collection events\0A                       actor_state_change - actor state change events\0A                       scheduler - basic scheduler events\0A                       scheduler_messaging - inter-scheduler messaging events\0A                       systematic_testing - systematic testing events\0A                       systematic_testing_details - detailed systematic testing events\0A                   categories can be enabled by comma separated glob patterns\0A                   defaults to all categories disabled\0A  --ponytracingforceactortracing\0A                   Force tracing for actors. Valid options are:\0A                       all - force tracing for all actors\0A                       cd_only - only force tracing for the cycle detector\0A                       none - do not force tracing for any actors\0A                   defaults to: none\0A  --ponytracingflightrecorderbuffer\0A                   Number of events to buffer per-thread in flight recorder mode\0A                   (will be rounded up to the nearest power of 2; min allowed is 1)\0A                   Defaults to 16384.\0A  --ponytracingflightrecorderhandletermint\0A                   Also trap on SIGINT (Ctrl-C) and SIGTERM in flight recorder mode.\0A  --ponypintracingthread\0A                   Pin the tracing thread to a CPU the way scheduler threads\0A                   are pinned to CPUs. Requires `--ponypin` to be set to have\0A                   any effect.\0A  --ponyversion    Print the version of the compiler and exit.\0A  --ponyhelp       Print the runtime usage options and exit.\0A\0ANOTE: These can be programmatically overridden. See the docstring in the\0A      `RuntimeOptions` struct in the `builtin` package.\00", align 1
